Hi @turabiyldrm

In conclusion, while converting to ONNX introduces an acceptable level of accuracy error, the execution time is reduced from 90 minutes to 23 minutes. Therefore, given the inference time constraints in the competition, we recommend using the ONNX version.

In detail, when the same audio waveform is input to both the Perch and ONNX models, the output Logits do not perfectly match, resulting in slight differences.

ex)
BC2026_Train_0001_S08_20250606_030007_5    0.10253045  0.20369478 <---ONNX Use(MyNotebook V15)
BC2026_Train_0001_S08_20250606_030007_5    0.102530636 0.2036958  <---ONNX Non Use(MyNotebook V14)

⇒Logit matches up to about 5 decimal places, but precision errors occur beyond that.
The main reasons for this are the following three
1. Graph Optimization and Operator Fusion Operator fusion combines three computational steps Conv, BatchNorm, and ReLU into a single mathematically equivalent formula. While mathematically equivalent, the order of calculations and the units used for fusion change in the program, resulting in slight discrepancies in floating-point calculation results.

2. Rounding Errors in Floating-Point Arithmetic (FP32) and Hardware Instruction Differences TensorFlow (native environment) and ONNX Runtime use different C++ mathematical libraries and CPU/GPU instruction sets (MKL, Eigen, cuDNN, etc.) in the backend. In deep learning, where floating-point (FP32) multiplication and addition are repeated millions of times, these library differences cause "rounding errors" that accumulate towards the final layer, resulting in slight differences in output values.

3. Differences in "Approximation" Algorithms for Specific Functions For transcendental functions such as Exp, Log, and GELU, and for image and audio resizing (interpolation) processing, TensorFlow and ONNX may employ slightly different approximation calculation algorithms.


